<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/intermediate/infection-and-antibiotics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Infectious disease medicine is <strong>a co-evolutionary arms race with fast-reproducing
    enemies</strong>. The antibiotic era (1940s→) turned once-fatal infections into inconveniences
    — and misuse has been breeding resistance ever since. Understanding bacteria versus viruses
    decides treatment; understanding selection pressure decides whether treatments keep working.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the enemy taxonomy, the weapons, and the resistance problem" widget="equationStep" config={infectCfg} />

  <h2>Epidemiology in one lesson's worth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>R₀ and herd immunity:</strong> if each case infects R₀ others, vaccination must exceed (R₀−1)/R₀ to stop spread — measles' R₀ of 12–18 demands ~95% immunity. The maths behind every outbreak response.</li>
    <li><strong>Semmelweis (1847):</strong> handwashing cut maternity-ward deaths dramatically — and was rejected for decades because it contradicted the era's theory. Evidence without acceptable mechanism got ignored; a permanent lesson in how medicine changes its mind.</li>
    <li><strong>Global health infrastructure matters:</strong> smallpox eradication required surveillance, ring vaccination and international coordination — the template that nearly finished polio and that contained SARS-1 (2003) and Ebola outbreaks.</li>
    <li><strong>Zoonoses are the frontier:</strong> ~75% of emerging infections come from animals — HIV, Ebola, COVID. Contact between humans and wildlife is the upstream risk factor for pandemics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fleming's penicillin papers; O'Neill, Review on Antimicrobial Resistance (2016); WHO eradication records; CDC principles of epidemiology.</p>
</TopicPage>
